=== layout: subsection

# 19. Drug Approval and Clinical Evidence

=== layout: bookend

# 19. Questions

@body

- What types of products are subject to clinical trials?
- What are biologics?
- How do biologics differ from drugs in their clinical trial processes?
- What are medical devices?
- How do medical devices differ from drugs in their clinical trial processes?
- Drug Approval: What are the criteria for a drug to pass or fail a clinical trial?
- What statistical methods are used to determine the efficacy of a drug?
- How is the risk-benefit ratio evaluated during clinical trials?

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. From Bench to Patients

@body

- Developers characterize the product, study nonclinical effects, and provide manufacturing and safety information before covered human research begins.
- An IND concerns investigational use of a drug or biologic. NDA and BLA are marketing-application pathways for drugs and biologics, respectively.
- The evidence must support the proposed population, dose, route, and indication.
- Permission to conduct a trial does not establish that the product is approved for sale.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/fdas-drug-review-process-ensuring-drugs-are-safe-and-effective

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Drugs, Biologics, and Devices

@body

- Biologics include products such as vaccines, therapeutic proteins, and cell therapies. Their manufacturing process and consistency can be central to product identity.
- Both drugs and biologics require appropriate evidence of safety and effectiveness; biologics do not bypass clinical evaluation.
- Device pathways depend on classification, intended use, and applicable requirements. Not every device follows drug-style phases or requires the same kind of clinical trial.
- Classify the actual product before drawing its development pathway.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/fdas-drug-review-process-ensuring-drugs-are-safe-and-effective

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Beyond Statistical Significance

@body

- A prespecified endpoint defines what the trial will measure. Randomization and appropriate controls help support a causal interpretation.
- Effect size describes how much changed; confidence intervals describe uncertainty. A small p-value does not establish a large or worthwhile benefit.
- Safety findings, missing data, multiple comparisons, and the clinical importance of the endpoint affect interpretation.
- Approval weighs benefits and risks for the intended use, including manufacturing quality and the credibility of the evidence.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/fdas-drug-review-process-ensuring-drugs-are-safe-and-effective

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Read a Simple Trial Result

@body

- Illustrative data: 20 of 100 control patients and 10 of 100 treated patients experience a specified event.
- The absolute reduction is 10 percentage points. The relative reduction is 50 percent.
- Both statements describe the same result, but they can create different impressions. Examine uncertainty, adverse effects, follow-up, and the severity of the event before judging value.
- These invented numbers teach interpretation; they are not evidence for an actual treatment.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/fdas-drug-review-process-ensuring-drugs-are-safe-and-effective

=== layout: big-image

@image

![19. Follow the Evidence through Development](assets/set6_drawings/trial_path.drawn.svg)

@caption

The diagram summarizes different study purposes. Optional exploratory work and combined phases can occur; no phase label guarantees success.

=== layout: subsection

# 20. Clinical Trial Phases

=== layout: bookend

# 20. Questions

@body

- Clinical research can include exploratory Phase 0 and Phases I, II, III, and IV; Phase 0 is optional.
- What specific objectives does each phase aim to achieve?
- What particular aspects are under investigation in each phase?
- How is the initial dosage determined in Phase 0/I, and how is it adjusted in subsequent phases?
- What criteria are used to select participants for each phase?
- How many participants are tested in each clinical trial phase?
- How are results scaled from a small cohort in early phases to a larger population in later phases?
- What is the average or expected duration of each phase?
- What are the typical costs for each phase?
- What is the likelihood of succeeding in each phase?
- In what situations might a clinical trial be halted prematurely, and why?
- What is your opinion of the cost of clinical trials?
- Are they too expensive and driving up the cost of medication?
- Or is that the cost of safety?

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Each Phase Has a Different Job

@body

- Phase 0 is an optional exploratory approach, often using very small exposures to study early pharmacology; it is not a mandatory step for every product.
- Phase I focuses on initial human safety, tolerability, and dose. Phase II explores activity, dosing, and further safety in people with the condition.
- Phase III evaluates benefit and risk in larger studies. Phase IV studies take place after approval.
- Some phases overlap or combine. The labels organize questions rather than guarantee a rigid five-step staircase.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research

=== layout: big-image

@image

![20. Read the Phase Staircase](assets/set6_sources/clinical_phase_staircase.png)

@caption

Phase 0 is optional. The staircase organizes study purposes; phases can overlap or combine. The icons are not actual sample sizes, and moving to the next phase is not guaranteed.

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Who Participates, and for How Long?

@body

- FDA's typical descriptions list roughly 20-100 people for Phase I, up to several hundred for Phase II, and about 300-3,000 for Phase III.
- Phase I may use healthy volunteers or patients. Oncology and other higher-risk contexts often require patients.
- Typical durations run from months in early studies to months or years in later studies, with variation by product and endpoint.
- Rare diseases, vaccines, cells, and long-term outcomes can require different designs and follow-up.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Dose Is a Scientific Question

@body

- Starting dose draws on the nonclinical safety package, pharmacology, expected human exposure, and the proposed population.
- Escalation uses planned rules and safety review; the highest tolerated dose is not always the best useful dose.
- Stopping can follow unacceptable toxicity, lack of benefit, convincing benefit under prespecified rules, or operational problems.
- Participant protection and interpretability belong in the protocol before the first result appears.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Why Published Estimates Differ

@body

- Per-patient cost, the cost of one trial, total clinical-program cost, and total discovery-to-approval investment are different quantities.
- A success rate depends on indication, modality, period, dataset, and the transition being measured.
- A published dollar amount or success percentage is a dated estimate for a defined dataset, not a universal current price or probability.
- Compare the cost of collecting useful evidence with the cost of approving an ineffective or harmful product. Faster recruitment is not a substitute for a valid endpoint.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research

=== layout: big-image

@image

![20. What the Body Does and What the Drug Does](assets/set6_sources/topic_20_101.png)

@caption

Pharmacokinetics follows exposure through the body. Pharmacodynamics concerns the effects associated with that exposure. A mechanistic link still needs testing against meaningful outcomes.

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Keep the Study Questions Separate

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Study | Central Question | What It Cannot Establish Alone |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Phase 0, when used | What happens at exploratory exposure? | A therapeutic benefit |
| Phase I | What dose and initial risks? | Effectiveness in all patients |
| Phase II | Is there activity and a useful dose? | A complete benefit-risk picture |
| Phase III | Does benefit justify risk in this use? | Absence of every rare harm |
| Phase IV | What happens after approval? | That every observed event was caused by the drug |

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/drug-development-process/step-3-clinical-research

=== layout: subsection

# 21. Orphan Drugs and Expedited Programs

=== layout: bookend

# 21. Questions

@body

- Designations: What impact does the "orphan drug" designation have on the clinical trial process?
- How does the FDA's "Breakthrough Therapy" designation impact clinical trial phases?
- What are the criteria for receiving a "Breakthrough Therapy" designation?
- Fast-Track: Are there expedited processes for drug approval?
- What qualifies a drug to be fast-tracked?
- How does the FDA's "Breakthrough Therapy" designation connect with the fast-track approval?
- How did the FDA's response to the AIDS crisis reform the approval process for life-threatening diseases?
- What implications does the use of accelerated approval mechanisms have for new medications?

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Orphan Status and Approval Differ

@body

- Orphan-drug designation supports development for rare diseases or conditions under the statutory criteria, generally fewer than 200,000 people in the United States or the applicable cost-recovery criterion.
- Incentives can include tax credits, fee relief, and potential orphan exclusivity following approval.
- Designation does not demonstrate that a drug is safe or effective and does not itself grant marketing approval.
- Small eligible populations create study-design challenges; they do not eliminate the need for credible evidence.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/learn-about-drug-and-device-approvals/fast-track-breakthrough-therapy-accelerated-approval-priority-review

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Four Names, Four Different Tools

@body

- Fast Track supports development and review for serious conditions with unmet need.
- Breakthrough Therapy requires preliminary clinical evidence of substantial improvement over available therapy on a clinically significant endpoint.
- Priority Review changes the review goal. Accelerated Approval can use a surrogate or intermediate endpoint under defined criteria.
- These tools can overlap, but they are not synonyms and do not guarantee approval.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/learn-about-drug-and-device-approvals/fast-track-breakthrough-therapy-accelerated-approval-priority-review

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. What Does the Surrogate Predict?

@body

- A surrogate such as a biomarker may change before survival, functioning, or symptoms can be evaluated.
- The key question is whether that change predicts a meaningful benefit in the particular setting.
- Confirmatory evidence and continuing oversight matter after accelerated approval; an approval can be withdrawn when the evidence does not support benefit or required conditions are not met.
- The AIDS crisis helped motivate faster access. Speed and uncertainty still need to be explained together.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/learn-about-drug-and-device-approvals/fast-track-breakthrough-therapy-accelerated-approval-priority-review

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Compare the Designations

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Program | What It Changes |
| --- | --- |
| Orphan designation | Incentives for qualifying rare-disease development |
| Fast Track | Development and review interactions for serious unmet needs |
| Breakthrough Therapy | Intensive support based on preliminary clinical evidence |
| Priority Review | The review goal for a marketing application |
| Accelerated Approval | The endpoint basis for approval under defined criteria |

None of these names alone establishes that a product is approved.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/patients/learn-about-drug-and-device-approvals/fast-track-breakthrough-therapy-accelerated-approval-priority-review

